(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["cerebral"]="<h1>Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-the-brain-can-t-wait-for-blood\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why the brain can't wait for blood</h2>\n<p>The brain is about 2% of body weight but uses about <strong>15% of the cardiac output</strong> and about <strong>20% of the body's oxygen</strong> at rest. Yet it stores almost no oxygen and very little glucose. So it depends on a <strong>continuous</strong> blood supply:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>About <strong>10 seconds</strong> without blood flow causes <strong>unconsciousness</strong>.</li>\n<li>A few minutes causes permanent damage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Not every part is equally fragile.</strong> The cerebral cortex, <strong>basal ganglia</strong> and <strong>thalamus</strong> are the most sensitive to low oxygen. The <strong>brainstem</strong>, which runs breathing and circulation, is more resistant. That is why a patient can survive a severe hypoxic injury with brainstem functions intact.</p>\n<p><strong>Fuel:</strong> glucose supplies about <strong>90%</strong> of the brain's energy. Brain cells take it up <strong>without needing insulin</strong>. So <strong>hypoglycaemia</strong> hits the brain fast: confusion, then <strong>convulsions</strong> and <strong>coma</strong>. In <strong>prolonged starvation</strong>, the brain switches partly to <strong>ketone bodies</strong>, made by the liver from fat.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says that in starvation the brain uses \"other substances (amino acids, lipids and proteins)\". More precisely, the main alternative fuel is ketone bodies, which the liver makes from fatty acids; ordinary fatty acids are not a major brain fuel.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-where-does-the-blood-come-from\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Where does the blood come from?</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Circle_of_Willis_en.svg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Circle_of_Willis_en.svg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Circle_of_Willis_en.svg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Circle_of_Willis_en.svg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Detailed inferior view of cerebral arterial anatomy showing vertebral and basilar arteries, internal carotids, anterior communicating arteries, posterior communicating arteries and branches of the Circle of Willis.\"><figcaption><strong>The real anatomical arrangement of the Circle of Willis.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Rhcastilhos, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Circle_of_Willis_en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Two pairs of arteries supply the brain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The two <strong>internal carotid arteries</strong> supply most of the front and middle of the brain.</li>\n<li>The two <strong>vertebral arteries</strong> join to form the <strong>basilar artery</strong>, which supplies the back of the brain and the brainstem.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the base of the brain, these connect in a ring: the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>. In principle, if one feeding artery narrows slowly, blood can come round the ring from the others. In practice, the ring is often incomplete or has thin segments, so it can't always compensate.</p>\n<p><strong>Beyond the ring, there is little backup.</strong> The cerebral arteries that leave the circle have <strong>few connections (anastomoses)</strong> with each other. So when one is blocked suddenly, the tissue it supplies has no other source, and it dies.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Artery</th>\n  <th>Main territory</th>\n  <th>Blockage typically affects</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Anterior cerebral (ACA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Inner (medial) surface of the frontal and parietal lobes</td>\n  <td>The opposite <strong>leg</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Middle cerebral (MCA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Most of the outer (lateral) surface of the hemisphere</td>\n  <td>The opposite <strong>face and arm</strong>; <strong>speech</strong>, if the dominant side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Posterior cerebral (PCA)</strong></td>\n  <td>Occipital lobe</td>\n  <td><strong>Vision</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>middle cerebral artery</strong> is a branch of the internal carotid. It is <strong>not</strong> part of the ring itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-does-the-brain-keep-its-blood-flow-steady\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> How does the brain keep its blood flow steady?</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure dg-figure\"><figcaption>Pressure autoregulation — conceptual blood-flow curve</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 274\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cerebral blood flow against mean arterial pressure. Flow rises at low pressure, stays nearly constant between about 60 and 150 mmHg, and rises again at higher pressure.\"><defs><marker id=\"ca-m\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head\"/></marker><marker id=\"ca-b\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-blue\"/></marker><marker id=\"ca-r\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-red\"/></marker><marker id=\"ca-g\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-gold\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M50 20V220H346\" class=\"dg-axis\"/><path d=\"M112 220V60M262 220V60\" class=\"dg-guide\"/><path d=\"M54 214C80 200 98 160 112 132C150 128 220 126 262 122C292 110 316 80 342 40\" class=\"dg-curve-blue\"/><text x=\"187\" y=\"84\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Autoregulated range</text><text x=\"187\" y=\"104\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">flow nearly constant</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"40\" class=\"dg-s\">Below it,</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"58\" class=\"dg-s\">flow falls</text><text x=\"272\" y=\"168\" class=\"dg-s\">Above it,</text><text x=\"272\" y=\"186\" class=\"dg-s\">flow rises</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"238\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈ 60</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"238\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈ 150</text><text x=\"198\" y=\"262\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mean arterial pressure (mmHg) →</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"120\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 22 120)\">Cerebral blood flow →</text></svg><p>As driving pressure changes, cerebral arterioles adjust resistance to buffer cerebral blood flow. The apparent plateau is approximate, not perfectly flat; its boundaries vary and can shift with disease or chronic hypertension.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Autoregulation.</strong> When perfusion pressure rises, the cerebral arterioles <strong>constrict</strong>; when it falls, they <strong>dilate</strong>. So cerebral blood flow stays nearly constant across a wide range of blood pressures (classically quoted as a <span data-term=\"map\">mean arterial pressure</span> of about 60–150 mmHg; the limits vary between people). Outside that range, or after a brain injury, flow simply follows pressure. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Control</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Carbon dioxide: the strongest controller.</strong> A rise in arterial CO₂ raises H⁺ around the vessels, and the arterioles <strong>dilate</strong>, so flow rises. A fall in CO₂ <strong>constricts</strong> them. That is why <strong>overbreathing</strong> (which blows off CO₂) can make you <strong>dizzy</strong>: cerebral blood flow falls. Severe hypoxia also dilates cerebral vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Active areas get more blood.</strong> When a brain region works harder, local signals dilate its arterioles, so flow rises <strong>just in that region</strong>. This is what brain-activity scans detect.</p>\n<p><strong>Nerves play only a small part.</strong> Cerebral vessels are supplied by <strong>sympathetic (adrenergic)</strong>, <strong>cholinergic</strong> and <strong>sensory</strong> nerves. The sensory nerves explain why the <strong>vessels and meninges feel pain</strong> (a cause of headache), although brain tissue itself does not.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which part of the brain is most resistant to a period of low oxygen?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The cerebral cortex, which controls conscious thought</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The basal ganglia, which help to control movement</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The brainstem, which controls breathing and circulation</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The thalamus, which relays sensory signals to the cortex</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:1df5bfa5\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are the most sensitive. The brainstem tolerates hypoxia better.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why can a sudden blockage of the middle cerebral artery kill brain tissue, even with a Circle of Willis?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Beyond the circle, cerebral arteries have few connections with each other</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The middle cerebral artery carries blood away from the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The Circle of Willis closes completely during a stroke</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The middle cerebral artery has no branches into the brain</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:8ecffeef\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The ring sits upstream. The arteries leaving it have little anastomosis, so a blocked branch leaves its territory without supply.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A student hyperventilates and feels dizzy. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. High CO₂ dilates the cerebral arterioles, so brain pressure rises</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low oxygen in the blood constricts the cerebral arterioles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Fast breathing raises blood pressure above the autoregulation range</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Low CO₂ constricts the cerebral arterioles, so brain blood flow falls</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:2b1161c9\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Overbreathing blows off CO₂. Cerebral arterioles constrict, and flow drops.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-a-rigid-box-intracranial-pressure-and-perfusion\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> A rigid box: intracranial pressure and perfusion</h2>\n<p><strong>The Monro–Kellie doctrine.</strong> The skull is a closed, rigid box. It holds three things whose total volume stays nearly constant:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brain</strong>, about 1400 g</li>\n<li><strong>Blood</strong>, about 75 mL</li>\n<li><strong>CSF</strong>, about 75 mL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If one increases (a bleed, a tumour, swelling), something else must leave. At first, CSF and venous blood are squeezed out, so the pressure barely rises. Once that reserve is used up, even a small extra volume makes the <strong>intracranial pressure (ICP)</strong> shoot up, and the rising pressure <strong>compresses the cerebral vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cerebral perfusion pressure: the pressure that actually drives blood through the brain.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>CPP = MAP − ICP</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal: MAP 90, ICP 10 → <strong>CPP 80 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n<li>ICP rises to 35, MAP still 90 → <strong>CPP 55 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The arm blood pressure hasn't changed, yet the brain is getting less blood.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure dg-figure\"><figcaption>Increasing ICP reduces the driving pressure available to perfuse the brain</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 232\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two bars at a mean arterial pressure of 90 mmHg. With ICP 10 mmHg, cerebral perfusion pressure is 80 mmHg. With ICP 35 mmHg, it falls to 55 mmHg.\"><text x=\"10\" y=\"20\" class=\"dg-s\">MAP 90 mmHg in both cases</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"52\" class=\"dg-b\">ICP 10 mmHg</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"62\" width=\"300\" height=\"32\" rx=\"8\" class=\"dg-blue\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"84\" class=\"dg-t\">CPP = 90 − 10 = 80 mmHg</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"130\" class=\"dg-b\">ICP 35 mmHg</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"140\" width=\"300\" height=\"32\" rx=\"8\" class=\"dg-ghost\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"140\" width=\"206\" height=\"32\" rx=\"8\" class=\"dg-red\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"162\" class=\"dg-t\">CPP = 90 − 35 = 55 mmHg</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"204\" class=\"dg-s\">CPP = MAP − ICP: the higher the ICP,</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"222\" class=\"dg-s\">the less pressure is left to perfuse the brain.</text></svg><p>Illustrative pressure calculation; these bars are not a patient-specific treatment threshold. Cerebral flow additionally depends on resistance.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The Cushing reflex: a last-ditch defence.</strong> When ICP climbs very high (the lecture gives more than about <strong>33 mmHg</strong>), the brainstem itself becomes ischaemic. Its <span data-term=\"vasomotor-centre\">vasomotor centre</span> fires a massive <strong>sympathetic</strong> discharge that <strong>raises arterial pressure</strong>, trying to force blood back in. The <span data-term=\"baroreceptors\">baroreceptors</span> detect the high pressure and <strong>slow the heart</strong>. <strong>Irregular breathing</strong> may follow.</p>\n<p>So the <strong>Cushing triad</strong> is <strong>high blood pressure, slow heart rate and irregular breathing</strong>. It is a <strong>late, dangerous sign</strong>: the brain is about to be compressed past its limit. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes\">→ Reflexes</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-the-coverings-the-ventricles-and-csf\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> The coverings, the ventricles and CSF</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meninges-en.svg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meninges-en.svg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meninges-en.svg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meninges-en.svg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Cross-sectional anatomical illustration identifying dura mater, arachnoid mater, subarachnoid space and pia mater\"><figcaption><strong>Meninges and the subarachnoid compartment.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Mysid and Wikimedia contributors, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meninges-en.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The meninges</strong>, from outside in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Dura mater:</strong> tough, attached to the skull.</li>\n<li><strong>Arachnoid mater:</strong> thin, joined to the brain surface by fine strands (trabeculae).</li>\n<li><strong>Subarachnoid space:</strong> contains <strong>CSF</strong> and the cerebral blood vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Pia mater:</strong> clings to the brain surface, following every fold.</li>\n</ol>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture says the subdural space also contains CSF. In normal anatomy, the subdural space is only a potential space; CSF lies in the subarachnoid space.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>Where CSF is made and where it goes.</strong> CSF is made mainly by the <strong>choroid plexus</strong> in the lateral, third and fourth ventricles. It then flows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Lateral ventricles</strong> → through the <strong>foramen of Monro</strong> (interventricular foramen) →</li>\n<li><strong>Third ventricle</strong> → through the <strong>aqueduct of Sylvius</strong> (cerebral aqueduct) →</li>\n<li><strong>Fourth ventricle</strong> → out through the <strong>foramen of Magendie</strong> (middle) and the <strong>foramina of Luschka</strong> (sides) →</li>\n<li><strong>Subarachnoid space</strong>, around the brain and spinal cord →</li>\n<li>Absorbed through the <strong>arachnoid villi</strong> into the venous sinuses, and so into the blood.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure dg-figure\"><figcaption>CSF route from ventricular production to subarachnoid circulation</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 440\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"CSF route, top to bottom: made mainly by the choroid plexus; lateral ventricles, through the foramen of Monro to the third ventricle, through the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle, through the foramina of Magendie and Luschka to the subarachnoid space, then absorbed through the arachnoid villi into the venous sinuses.\"><defs><marker id=\"cs-m\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head\"/></marker><marker id=\"cs-b\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-blue\"/></marker><marker id=\"cs-r\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-red\"/></marker><marker id=\"cs-g\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-gold\"/></marker></defs><text x=\"180\" y=\"18\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Made mainly by the choroid plexus</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"30\" width=\"220\" height=\"40\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"56\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lateral ventricles</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"120\" width=\"220\" height=\"40\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"146\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Third ventricle</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"210\" width=\"220\" height=\"40\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"236\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fourth ventricle</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"300\" width=\"220\" height=\"40\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-blue\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"326\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Subarachnoid space</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"390\" width=\"220\" height=\"40\" rx=\"10\" class=\"dg-green\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"416\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Venous sinuses (blood)</text><path d=\"M150 72V116\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#cs-m)\"/><text x=\"162\" y=\"100.0\" class=\"dg-s\">foramen of Monro</text><path d=\"M150 162V206\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#cs-m)\"/><text x=\"162\" y=\"190.0\" class=\"dg-s\">cerebral aqueduct</text><path d=\"M150 252V296\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#cs-m)\"/><text x=\"162\" y=\"271.0\" class=\"dg-s\">foramina of Magendie</text><text x=\"162\" y=\"289.0\" class=\"dg-s\">and Luschka</text><path d=\"M150 342V386\" class=\"dg-line\" marker-end=\"url(#cs-m)\"/><text x=\"162\" y=\"361.0\" class=\"dg-s\">absorbed through</text><text x=\"162\" y=\"379.0\" class=\"dg-s\">arachnoid villi</text></svg><p>Original conceptual diagram; arrows show the direction of bulk flow, not anatomical proportions.</p></figure>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>CSF value</th>\n  <th>Typical adult</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Volume</td>\n  <td>About <strong>150 mL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Production</td>\n  <td>About <strong>550 mL/day</strong>, so it is replaced several times a day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pressure (lumbar, lying down)</td>\n  <td><strong>70–180 mm CSF</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Protein</td>\n  <td>About <strong>20 mg/dL</strong>, far less than plasma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Glucose</td>\n  <td>About <strong>64 mg/dL</strong>, roughly two-thirds of blood glucose</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What CSF does:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cushions</strong> the brain against blows to the head.</li>\n<li><strong>Buoyancy:</strong> a brain weighing about 1400 g in air weighs only about <strong>50 g</strong> in CSF, so it doesn't crush its own base.</li>\n<li><strong>Removes waste:</strong> the one-way flow into the blood carries metabolites and drugs away.</li>\n<li><strong>Carries hormones</strong> to distant parts of the brain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hydrocephalus: when CSF builds up.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-communicating (obstructive):</strong> something <strong>blocks the pathway</strong> inside the ventricles, for example at the narrow aqueduct. The ventricles upstream of the block swell.</li>\n<li><strong>Communicating:</strong> the ventricles connect normally, but the <strong>arachnoid villi can't absorb</strong> CSF fast enough.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lumbar puncture</strong> samples CSF from the <strong>subarachnoid space in the lower back</strong>, below where the spinal cord ends. If <span data-term=\"icp\">ICP</span> may be raised by a mass, it must be checked first, because releasing pressure from below can pull the brain downwards.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 4–5\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–5 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A patient's MAP is 90 mmHg and ICP rises from 10 to 40 mmHg. What happens to cerebral perfusion pressure?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It stays at 80 mmHg, because autoregulation keeps it constant</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls from 80 to 50 mmHg, although arm blood pressure is unchanged</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It rises from 80 to 130 mmHg, because ICP adds to the MAP</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It falls to 40 mmHg, because CPP always equals ICP</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:d0fba9ac\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> CPP = MAP − ICP: 90 − 10 = 80, then 90 − 40 = 50 mmHg.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> In the Cushing reflex, why does the heart rate fall?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The vagus nerve is crushed directly by the swollen brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Raised ICP blocks sympathetic nerves to the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The heart receives less blood when ICP is high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The baroreceptors respond to the sympathetic rise in blood pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:2a5ddb19\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Brainstem ischaemia drives blood pressure up. The baroreflex then slows the heart.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A tumour blocks the aqueduct of Sylvius. Which ventricles enlarge?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The lateral and third ventricles, upstream of the block</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The fourth ventricle only, downstream of the block</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. All four ventricles equally, because CSF absorption fails</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. None, because CSF can bypass the aqueduct easily</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:1bd227a3\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> CSF made in the lateral and third ventricles can't get past the aqueduct, so they swell: non-communicating hydrocephalus.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"6-the-blood-brain-barrier\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> The blood–brain barrier</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blood_brain_barrier.png?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blood_brain_barrier.png?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blood_brain_barrier.png?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blood_brain_barrier.png?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Comparison of brain capillary endothelial barrier with the more permeable capillaries of other tissues\"><figcaption><strong>What makes cerebral capillaries different?</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">David Nascari and Alan Sved, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blood_brain_barrier.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>In most capillaries, small molecules slip through gaps between the endothelial cells. In the brain, the endothelial cells are sealed together by <strong>tight junctions</strong>, and there are no gaps or pores. So anything leaving the blood must pass <strong>through</strong> the cells. This is the <strong>blood–brain barrier</strong>, and it lies in the <strong>capillary endothelium</strong>. Surrounding <span data-term=\"pericytes\">pericytes</span> and astrocyte endfeet help maintain it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Crosses easily</th>\n  <th>Crosses poorly</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Water, O₂, CO₂</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Proteins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Lipid-soluble</strong> substances (e.g. steroid hormones)</td>\n  <td>Substances <strong>bound to plasma proteins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Glucose</strong>, quickly, but only by a <strong>carrier</strong> (GLUT1)</td>\n  <td>Most <strong>ions and water-soluble</strong> molecules</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why have it?</strong> It keeps the neurons' environment <strong>constant</strong>, despite swings in blood ions and hormones, and <strong>shields</strong> them from toxins and circulating antigens.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A drug acting on the brain must be able to <strong>cross</strong> the barrier.</li>\n<li>The barrier <strong>breaks down</strong> in infection, injury, tumours and inflammation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong><span data-term=\"choroid-plexus\">choroid plexus</span></strong> has its own barrier between blood and CSF, but there the seal is formed by the <strong>epithelial cells</strong>, not the capillaries.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-stroke\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Stroke</h2>\n<p>A <strong>stroke</strong> is a sudden loss of blood supply to part of the brain, causing ischaemia and cell death. The signs depend on which artery is affected (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cerebral?section=2-where-does-the-blood-come-from\">§2</a>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Ischaemic stroke</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Haemorrhagic stroke</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>What happens</td>\n  <td>An artery is <strong>blocked</strong></td>\n  <td>An artery <strong>ruptures</strong> and bleeds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Causes</td>\n  <td><strong>Atherosclerotic plaque</strong>, <strong>thrombus</strong> or <strong>embolus</strong></td>\n  <td>Often a ruptured <strong>aneurysm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Extra danger</td>\n  <td>—</td>\n  <td>The blood adds volume, so <strong>ICP rises</strong> (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cerebral?section=4-a-rigid-box-intracranial-pressure-and-perfusion\">§4</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 6–7\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 6–7 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Where does the blood–brain barrier lie?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. In the arachnoid villi that absorb the cerebrospinal fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. In the pia mater that covers the surface of the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. In the tight junctions of the brain's capillary endothelium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. In the smooth muscle wall of the cerebral arterioles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:63dcf042\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Endothelial cells sealed by tight junctions force substances to pass through the cells, not between them.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does glucose cross the blood–brain barrier?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Slowly, by diffusing between the endothelial cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Quickly, by a specific carrier in the endothelial cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Only when insulin opens channels in the endothelium</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Bound to plasma proteins that carry it across</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:2fa68078\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Glucose is water-soluble, so it needs a carrier (GLUT1). The carrier moves it fast, and no insulin is needed.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can a haemorrhagic stroke damage brain tissue far from the bleeding artery?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The bleeding artery stops all flow through the Circle of Willis</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The blood dissolves the blood–brain barrier throughout the brain</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The bleed lowers the CSF pressure, so the brain collapses</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The blood adds volume inside the skull, raising ICP everywhere</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cerebral:74b3dbf4\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> In a rigid skull, extra volume raises ICP, lowering perfusion pressure across the whole brain.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The brain can't store oxygen:</strong> about 10 seconds without flow causes unconsciousness. The cortex, basal ganglia and thalamus are most sensitive; the brainstem is more resistant.</li>\n<li><strong>Fuel:</strong> glucose (about 90%), taken up without insulin. Prolonged starvation: ketone bodies.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply:</strong> internal carotids + vertebrals → basilar, joined in the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>. Beyond the ring, <strong>little anastomosis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> keeps flow steady across a wide pressure range. <strong>CO₂</strong> is the strongest controller: high CO₂ dilates, low CO₂ constricts.</li>\n<li><strong>Monro–Kellie:</strong> brain + blood + CSF in a rigid skull. <strong>CPP = MAP − ICP.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cushing reflex:</strong> very high ICP → brainstem ischaemia → hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing. A late sign.</li>\n<li><strong>CSF:</strong> made by the choroid plexus; lateral → Monro → third → Sylvius → fourth → Magendie/Luschka → subarachnoid space → arachnoid villi. About 150 mL, about 550 mL/day.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrocephalus:</strong> non-communicating (pathway blocked) or communicating (absorption fails).</li>\n<li><strong>Blood–brain barrier:</strong> tight junctions in the capillary endothelium. Water, gases and lipid-soluble substances cross; glucose crosses by carrier; proteins don't.</li>\n<li><strong>Stroke:</strong> ischaemic (blocked) or haemorrhagic (ruptured, often an aneurysm).</li>\n</ol>\n";
